# Chapter 1: Girl hits boy
<div class="poem">


Wheels burning, ever churning, a Maiden of Light yet yearning. 

Before her, Man of Sorrow lay, now arrived his numbered day.

As she aims her Chariot True, Fetter’s mind has yet its due. 

By helm his riven future sprawled, shattered glass his thread enthralled.

With Sorrow spared by padded blow, common fellow now in stow.

Thread rimple, answer simple, she lay gun at Sorrow’s temple.


</div>



Steel rammed Max's spine. Glass shattered against his chest. His blood flicked across the dash before he landed in a leather seat. Neurons fired throughout his body, screaming about the damage being done but it never reached his brain.

Where was his skateboard?

Iridescent blonde hair wove through the crystal shards. All the colors of the evening sky faded as the hair swept apart. Celeste-blue stared back at him. A woman sat primly in a bucket seat across the cabin with skin as white as the plain t-shirt she wore. A fine brow swept downward to a button nose as though chiseled from marble. One hand gripped the steering wheel, the other glinted. Light stabbed his retinas.

As though performed thousands of times, her hand flowed upward and pointed a gun directly at max's mouth. Her finger turned white against the trigger. A light kindled around her head, spreading scintillating energy through every strand of hair until a golden blaze formed a ring encompassing her. Then her hair plunged to darkness. Two lights flashed to either side. Glass exploded around her. The door crumpled. Her seatbelt tightened against her chest. Gravity ripped the seat from under him and sent him tumbling toward her.

 

<div class="poem">



Gun’s roar crying, acrid scent rising, cabin now capsizing

Swirling chaos, man unbound, dumping twirling, his mind confound.

Maiden fastened, flailing still, chariot rolls against her will.

Blades collide, Errant Steel screeches, coppered fluid, man’s head breaches.

Severed thread and sorrow swell, upon us now the Final Knell.

Harrowed Man’s blight, amidst prism light, pierced by Maiden of Spite.



</div>



Blood and oil caressed Runa's nostrils. Screaming sang to her ears. Her body hung sideways, barely suspended by the seat belt with an arm hooked inside the steering wheel. Amidst the fading blackness, Runa caught his verdant green eyes.


Below her, a young man was splayed as if floating across the cabin's door. Ahead, the road flowed from the truck's side. A brown eighteen-wheeler sat crumpled across the street, a crowd slowly gathering around its smashed windshield.

Runa pressed against the door, stopping at the creak of metal. She unbuckled the seatbelt and eased down the sideways cabin. Crimson pooled beneath the young man's head. His breath rasped. She grasped for her pistol, her fingers tapping only leather. Only the ghost of stippling burned against her palm. Her forearm clenched and knuckles cracked.

She left the young man, stepping through the shattered windshield. Packages littered the intersection. Her box-shaped delivery truck rested on its side, scraped steel shining through blue paint. The evening breeze carried a coppered tinge of rot. She turned to look at the man, crumpled in his blood.

Glass crunched beneath her feet. She unknotted a glimmering red sash at her waist before gliding to the young man's side. Blood poured from the hole in his shoulder, pooling against his brown hair. She stooped next to him and bound his shoulder with the sash.

Her gaze slid outward, past the panicking mass, to the black fluid dripping down the other truck's windshield. She thrust her arm outward, pointing at a middle-aged woman.

"Ambulance. Now!" Runa shouted.


<div class="scene-change" ><img src="/images/books/valkyriextruck/bw-tiremark.webp" alt="tire mark" /></div>

A tug at Runa's hair drew her from dreamless slumber. She leaped from her seat, scanning the room—a closed door, pristine tile, wardrobe casting shadows across the wall-mounted television. Her hand darted to the holster, fingers grasping at empty air. She clenched her fist. Quick beeping bit her ears.

Max laid on his side, gripping the railing, hand nearing the hospital bed’s remote. She lashed out, grabbed his wrist and jerked it away. 

“Sleep,” she stated, pinning his arm to his side.

His eyes widened. Lines darted across the monitor, beeping frantically. Pallor spread across his face. His lids slipped over his green eyes. His panicked breath steadied, and he fell unconscious. She rolled him back to the center of the hospital bed, covering him with the blanket. Her hand moved to her waist, fingers tapping the top of her holster. Her gaze traveled to the red dot on the unconscious man's shoulder, slowly growing in size.

She slid her eyes to the exit and slipped a phone from her pocket. Her lips curled as they always had, imperceptibly. Dark text flickered against a light background as she read the poem again.


<div class="poem">


Shield-Swan lost, life-blade parted.

Board-Rider tossed, Weavers’s work dark-hearted.

Black death waxes, powers aligned

Shield-brother axes she must find

Ware venom-lace at Shield-Swan’s feet, 

Lest Lord’s embrace should she meet.

</div>



The pistol's stippling still lingered in her hand. She replaced the phone then flowed onto the chair. Max groaned, drawing her gaze to the bed, to the red dot seeping through his gown. She exhaled, cradling her face in interlocked fingers. 

“Skoldr,” she muttered.

The bed blurred, taking the young man and hospital with it. She didn't even notice the door swing open.

A red-haired man in blue smock man pushed a medical cart into the room. “Hello, Runa,” he said, pulling along side the bed. “Any change?”

She snapped out of her trance. "He woke," she replied and her gaze returned to the phone on the bed. 

"Great news. How was he feeling?" the nurse asked, leaning to change the IV bags. 

"He'll be fine," Runa stated. She leaned against seat, crossing her arms, silently scrutinizing the nurse. 

"He certainly is, especially with you watching over him," the nurse said before winking at her. 

She watched as he moved to Max’s injured side, stripping away the bandage covering his shoulder. The nurse poked and prodded, unwinding the old, then winding the new bandages before putting away his tools. "If he wakes again, you need to come get us. The doctor has a lot of questions about that injury," he said, pointing at Max’s shoulder. 

Runa nodded before looking out the window. The setting sun cast purple across the sleepy town. She stood. “I'm leaving, anyway. Goodnight.”

"Okay, we'll see you tomorrow," the nurse replied and left the room. Runa took one last look at the injured man before collecting her phone and leaving.




<div class="scene-change" ><img src="/images/books/valkyriextruck/bw-tiremark.webp" alt="tire mark" /></div>




The city's glow streaked past the newly installed windshield. Runa tapped a rhythm on her leather holster, keeping the beat of her hum. Her gaze wandered her truck's cabin. Twisted metal. Warped dashboard. She leaned on the armrest, feeling the jagged edge of torn steel. 

She caressed the wheel with a thumb.

An unlit gas station yawned to one side, across from a flowing chain link fence. A glint reflected off a bent exhaust. To the right, orange lights cast a glow across a row of destroyed cars. The brown truck rested in their center, the trailers now detached. The crash bar pressed into the radiator with headlights bowed inward like a catcher’s mitt. She stepped out of her vehicle, and glanced to either side. 

The street stood empty with only a sour and salty breeze accompanying her. She leaped the fence, landing gracefully onto gravel, and slunk toward her destination. 

Runa popped open the driver’s door with a yank. The dashboard bowed outward, like an unsightly blister. She climbed over the twisted metal and plastic, scanning every crevice.  Nothing.

She growled, her brow furrowing like her lips curled, invisible. “Why won't she respond?” She pressed on the dashboard, unbending it just enough to open the glove compartment. A Glock. She withdrew from the cabin, leaving the weapon behind her. She stepped from the semi. “Two wee—”

A cragged hand seized her leg. She crashed to the ground, slamming her head against the loose pebbles. Rocks scraped her exposed flesh. A pungent smell of septic rot flooded the air as her assailant came into view. 

A gnarled hand, writhing like a living branch, dragged her leg below the vehicle. A bubbling mass of arms and faces twisted beneath the undercarriage, silhouetted by erin-green flame. She kicked, catching her free leg on the truck’s step. Bracing against it, she jumped, propelling herself away from the vehicle and dragging the creature into the amber light. 

The jagged-edged flesh of the creature’s curling face clamped inward. Serrated appendages resembling teeth dug into her calf. Runa screamed. A head crawled up her thigh. Her other foot slammed downward, popping the stone head. Arms spread out of the place where the face had been. The mass propelled forward, scrambling with heads and limbs stretching toward her. Faces ballooned and burst with appendages protruding from the resulting cavities. Tendril-like arms slathered out from its charred mass, clawing and climbing toward her. Frantically, she kicked the hand binding her leg, breaking it free of the torrent of grasping limbs. With a flick of her arms, she propelled herself upright and sprinted toward her truck. Her throbbing leg screamed with every step. She glanced over her shoulder. The mass folded backward, then swelled, sputtering green flames over the gravel. A hand slithered along the ground grasping at her feet. 

Runa leaped the fence. Her injured leg buckled, sending her tumbling against the vehicle. 

She grasped at the truck’s handle, head twisting toward the now still yard. She brushed an errant strand of hair behind an ear, squinting at the amber-lit cars. Rot clung to her nose. With a yank, the door popped open and she slid inside.



